# React 19 Patterns and Best Practices

This project uses **React 19** with functional components and hooks.

## Component Structure

### Functional Components

```typescript
import { type FC } from "react";

interface MyComponentProps {
  title: string;
  count?: number;
}

export const MyComponent: FC<MyComponentProps> = ({ title, count = 0 }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Component Organization

```typescript
// 1. Imports
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";
import { api } from "~/utils/api";
import { Button } from "~/components/ui/button";

// 2. Types/Interfaces
interface Props {
  // ...
}

// 3. Component
export function MyComponent({ prop }: Props) {
  // 4. Hooks (in order: context, state, queries, effects, callbacks)
  const [state, setState] = useState(0);
  const { data } = api.example.useQuery();

  useEffect(() => {
    // Effect logic
  }, []);

  const handleClick = () => {
    // Handler logic
  };

  // 5. Early returns
  if (!data) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  // 6. Render
  return (
    <div>
      {/* JSX */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Hooks

### State Management

```typescript
// Simple state
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

// Object state
const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);

// Derived state (prefer useMemo if expensive)
const doubleCount = count * 2;
const expensiveValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensive(data), [data]);
```

### Effects

```typescript
// Run once on mount
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []);

// Run when dependencies change
useEffect(() => {
  updateData(id);
}, [id]);

// Cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = subscribe();
  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();
}, []);
```

### Custom Hooks

Place custom hooks in [src/hooks/](mdc:src/hooks/):

```typescript
// src/hooks/use-example.ts
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

export function useExample(param: string) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Logic
  }, [param]);

  return { value, setValue };
}

// Usage
import { useExample } from "~/hooks/use-example";

const { value, setValue } = useExample("test");
```

## Performance Optimization

### Memoization

```typescript
// Memoize expensive computations
const result = useMemo(() => expensiveOperation(data), [data]);

// Memoize callbacks passed to children
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]);

// Memoize components (use sparingly)
const MemoizedChild = memo(ChildComponent);
```

### Code Splitting

```typescript
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";

// Load component dynamically
const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import("~/components/HeavyComponent"), {
  loading: () => <div>Loading...</div>,
  ssr: false, // Disable SSR if needed
});
```

## Conditional Rendering

```typescript
// Conditional content
{isLoading && <Spinner />}
{error && <Alert>{error.message}</Alert>}
{data && <DataDisplay data={data} />}

// Ternary
{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />}

// Early return
if (!user) return <Login />;
return <Dashboard user={user} />;
```

## Lists and Keys

```typescript
// Always use unique, stable keys
{items.map((item) => (
  <Item key={item.id} {...item} />
))}

// ❌ Don't use index as key if list can change
{items.map((item, index) => (
  <Item key={index} {...item} />
))}
```

## Event Handlers

```typescript
// Inline handler (for simple cases)
<Button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</Button>

// Named handler (preferred for complex logic)
const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  // Submit logic
};

<form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>...</form>

// Passing arguments
<Button onClick={() => deleteItem(item.id)}>Delete</Button>
```

## Props

### Prop Destructuring

```typescript
// ✅ Destructure in function signature
function MyComponent({ title, count = 0 }: Props) {
  return <div>{title}: {count}</div>;
}

// ❌ Avoid accessing props.x everywhere
function MyComponent(props: Props) {
  return <div>{props.title}: {props.count}</div>;
}
```

### Children Prop

```typescript
interface Props {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

export function Container({ children }: Props) {
  return <div className="container">{children}</div>;
}
```

### Spreading Props

```typescript
// Pass all props to child
<ChildComponent {...props} />

// Separate specific props
const { className, ...rest } = props;
<div className={cn("base", className)} {...rest} />
```

## Forms

Use react-hook-form with zod validation:

```typescript
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  password: z.string().min(8),
});

type FormData = z.infer<typeof schema>;

export function LoginForm() {
  const form = useForm<FormData>({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema),
    defaultValues: {
      email: "",
      password: "",
    },
  });

  const onSubmit = (data: FormData) => {
    // Handle submission
  };

  return <form onSubmit={form.handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>...</form>;
}
```

## Best Practices

### Do's ✅

- Use functional components and hooks
- Destructure props in function signature
- Use TypeScript types for all props
- Extract complex logic to custom hooks
- Use meaningful component and variable names
- Keep components small and focused
- Use React Query (via tRPC) for server state
- Handle loading and error states
- Provide accessible components (use shadcn/ui components)

### Don'ts ❌

- Don't use class components (unless necessary)
- Don't mutate state directly - use setState
- Don't use index as key for dynamic lists
- Don't create functions inside render without useCallback (if passed as props)
- Don't fetch data in useEffect when tRPC is available
- Don't ignore TypeScript errors
- Don't create massive components - break them down
- Don't forget cleanup in useEffect

## Error Boundaries

For error boundaries, create a class component (or use a library):

```typescript
import { Component, type ReactNode } from "react";

interface Props {
  children: ReactNode;
  fallback?: ReactNode;
}

interface State {
  hasError: boolean;
}

export class ErrorBoundary extends Component<Props, State> {
  constructor(props: Props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return this.props.fallback ?? <div>Something went wrong</div>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

## React 19 Features

React 19 introduces new features. Use them when appropriate:

- Actions and form handling improvements
- use() hook for reading resources
- Improved hydration error messages
- Better TypeScript support
